    concepts: [
      { id: 'sre-1', term: 'SRE Principles', summary: 'Google-inspired approach to reliability as software.', detail: 'SRE treats operations as software problems: automate toil, measure reliability, and use error budgets to balance velocity versus stability. SREs write code to replace manual work, enforce consistency, and scale systems without scaling headcount. The toil cap typically limits manual work to 50%.', category: 'Cloud' },
      { id: 'sre-2', term: 'Error Budgets', summary: 'Acceptable unreliability budget from SLOs.', detail: 'An error budget equals 100% minus SLO. If your SLO is 99.9%, budget is 0.1%. Exhaust it early and the team freezes risky changes until reliability recovers. The budget converts reliability from moral argument to measurable trade-off aligned with product risk tolerance.', category: 'Cloud' },
      { id: 'sre-3', term: 'Toil Reduction', summary: 'Eliminate repetitive manual work through automation.', detail: 'Toil is manual, repetitive, tactical work that scales linearly. SREs target automation: deployments, rotations, incident response, capacity tasks. Toil is measured, reported, and reduced over time. If a human repeats it more than twice, automate it.', category: 'Cloud' },
      { id: 'sre-4', term: 'Incident Management', summary: 'Detect, respond, resolve, learn.', detail: 'Incidents require clear roles: incident commander, communications lead, operations lead. Detect with monitoring and paging. Respond with runbooks and checklists. Resolve by restoring service quickly, then investigate later. Postmortems must be blameless and produce action items.', category: 'Cloud' },
      { id: 'sre-5', term: 'Blameless Postmortems', summary: 'Learning-focused reviews after incidents.', detail: 'Blameless postmortems separate human error from system design. They ask what conditions allowed the mistake instead of who caused it. Outputs are timeline, root cause, remediation steps, and follow-up tickets. An organization that blames learns nothing; one that fixes systems improves.', category: 'Cloud' },
      { id: 'sre-6', term: 'Capacity Planning', summary: 'Predicting traffic and resource needs ahead of time.', detail: 'Capacity planning tracks growth trends, seasonality, marketing launches, and dependency constraints. Plan headroom based on peak-plus margin, not current average. Use budgets, quotas, and load testing to validate. Failure to plan causes throttling, throttling causes outages.', category: 'Cloud' },
      { id: 'sre-7', term: 'Change Management', summary: 'Reduce risk from deployments and config pushes.', detail: 'Changes are the top cause of incidents. Manage risk with canary releases, progressive rollouts, feature flags, and guarded rollbacks. Rate-limit changes during high-traffic periods. Deep change management correlates changes with anomalies to catch regressions fast.', category: 'Cloud' },
      { id: 'sre-8', term: 'Alerting Hygiene', summary: 'Actionable alerts, clear severity, and healthy on-call.', detail: 'Good alerting is low-noise, actionable, and tied to runbooks. Page only when human action is needed now. Use severity tiers: page, ticket, chatops. Track mean time to acknowledge and mean time to resolve. Protect on-call from alert storms with routing and maintenance windows.', category: 'Cloud' },
      { id: 'sre-9', term: 'User-Facing Reliability', summary: 'Design systems around real user experience.', detail: 'Reliability means users can accomplish their goals. Use synthetic probes from real user locations, canary traffic, and client-side metrics to measure true availability. Backend health is not enough; frontend errors, API regressions, and CDN failures all reduce user-facing reliability.', category: 'Cloud' },
      { id: 'sre-10', term: 'SRE Culture & Career', summary: 'Collaboration between dev and ops with shared incentives.', detail: 'SRE culture rewards production engagement without penalty for reasonable failure. Share on-call across developers and SREs. Measure production stories alongside feature work. Career ladders value coding, architecture, and operational rigor equally rather than rewarding heroics after outages.', category: 'Cloud' },
      { id: 'site-reliability-engineering-c11', term: 'SLI, SLO, SLA', summary: 'Measuring and promising reliability.', detail: 'SLIs are indicators; SLOs are internal reliability targets; SLAs are external contractual promises. Align SLOs to user expectations, not maximums. Missing an SLO triggers error budget decisions.', category: 'Cloud' },
      { id: 'site-reliability-engineering-c14', term: 'Reliability Testing', summary: 'Chaos engineering, load testing, and game days.', detail: 'Test real failure modes with controlled experiments: shutdown AZs, saturate dependencies, inject latency. Game days rehearse responses. Regular testing prevents monotony and uncovers hidden technical debt before outages.', category: 'Cloud' },
    ],
